@page "/reviews"
@attribute [Authorize]
@inject IReviewService Verdicts
@inject IDeliveryService Parcels
@inject ICurrentUser Caller
@inject IStringLocalizer<UiText> Localizer
@inherits PagedScreen<ListReviewsQuery, object, ReviewSort>

@* FR-62 to FR-67: reviews, and the three different screens the one route is for.

   A client sees what they wrote and a way to write another. A dispatcher sees every review and no
   way to touch one. An administrator sees every review and moderates them. A driver sees a
   sentence saying this is not theirs - they are the party being judged, and every review route
   refuses them.

   [Authorize] carries no roles on purpose, and this is the capability where a Roles argument would
   be most wrong: the five routes behind this screen are reserved to three different sets of
   callers, so one attribute could only ever state one of them, wrongly (AD-2, FR-12). Who may do
   what is IAccessGuard's answer inside IReviewService, and it runs whether or not a button was
   rendered.

   Independent @if blocks and one literal localizer key per string, for the two reasons the shell
   components already state: an `else` at markup level reads to NFR-14's scan as a rendered English
   word, and a key composed at run time is a key the closed-catalogue test cannot see.

   The role predicates are ReviewViews' rather than expressions here, so a rule about who sees what
   is a thing a test can read back rather than a condition buried in markup. *@

<PageTitle>@Localizer["ReviewsTitle"]</PageTitle>

@* The screen's title and, for a client, the one action that opens an empty form - on one line, the
   shape the four fleet screens and the two delivery ones already settled. *@
<div class="dt-page-head mb-4">
    <h1 class="dt-type-h1">
        <Icon Name="IconName.Reviews" />@Localizer["ReviewsTitle"]
    </h1>

    @* Offered only when there is something to review. An action whose form opens on an empty picker
       posts no delivery and is answered "no such delivery" - a true sentence about a question the
       client did not ask. The gate travels with the action into the head, as MyDeliveries wraps its
       own head action: the head holds the title and whatever the role in front of it may do, and
       for the three roles that may do nothing it holds the title alone. The hint below says the
       same thing the picker's own hint says, because it is the same rule: a finished delivery of
       theirs that they have not already written about. *@
    @if (_authors && _reviewable.Count > 0)
    {
        <button type="button" class="btn btn-primary dt-review-create" @onclick="OpenCreateAsync">
            <Icon Name="IconName.Create" />@Localizer["WriteReview"]
        </button>
    }
</div>

@* NFR-3: the message is the localized string for the code the service threw, resolved through the
   same catalogue the REST envelope uses. Nothing an exception carries reaches this page.

   Stood down while the form is open, because the dialog renders the same list (NFR-19). Two
   role="alert" regions holding one refusal is one refusal announced twice; exactly one of them is
   in the DOM at any moment, and which one it is follows where the reader is. *@
@if (!_formOpen)
{
    <DtFailureBanner Failures="_failures" InlineFields="@InlineFields" />
}

@* The sibling of the action above rather than a second copy of it: never both, and neither while
   the lists are still loading - the action waits for a delivery to put in its picker and this waits
   for `!Loading`, so the first pass shows a bare head and the sentence appears once the answer is
   in. Saying "there is nothing to review" before looking would be a claim the screen cannot yet
   make. It stays below the head because it is a sentence rather than a control - a paragraph inside
   the heading row would sit on the line beside the title. *@
@if (_authors && !Loading && _reviewable.Count == 0)
{
    <p class="dt-review-nothing-to-review">@Localizer["ReviewDeliveryHint"]</p>
}

@* `dt-table-cards` is where this screen opts into the shared collapse: below the phone breakpoint
   the two tables' rows stop being rows and become cards. The collapse is stated once, in
   `_theme.scss`, so nothing about it is written here or in this screen's own stylesheet - and
   because it is a global rule rather than a scoped one, none of it needs a `::deep`.

   The wrapper's own name stays beside it. `Reviews.razor.css` hangs this screen's per-cell label
   rules off it, and the phone-layout suite reads it as the element the table has to be inside.

   One wrapper around both tables rather than one each. No role is ever shown both, and the two
   take identical treatment, so a second wrapper would be a second opt-in and a second copy of the
   label rule, and the place a divergence between the author's cards and the moderator's would
   start. *@
<div class="dt-reviews dt-table-cards">
    @* FR-63: the author's own list. A separate table from the moderation one below rather than the
       same table with columns hidden, because the two are different types - AuthoredReviewSummary
       has no party field at all, so a driver's identity cannot reach this table however it is later
       edited (AD-17). *@
    @if (_authors)
    {
        <DtDataTable TItem="AuthoredReviewSummary"
                     Page="_minePage"
                     OnPage="GoToPageAsync"
                     OnPageSize="ResizeAsync"
                     CountOne="@Localizer["ReviewsCountOne"]"
                     CountMany="@Localizer["ReviewsCountMany"]"
                     IsLoading="Loading"
                     EmptyText="@Localizer["ReviewsEmpty"]">
            <HeaderTemplate>
                <th scope="col" aria-sort="@SortState(ReviewSort.Delivery)">
                    <button type="button" class="btn btn-link btn-sm" @onclick="SortHandler(ReviewSort.Delivery)">
                        @Localizer["ReviewDelivery"]<Icon Name="@SortGlyph(ReviewSort.Delivery)" />
                    </button>
                </th>
                <th scope="col" aria-sort="@SortState(ReviewSort.Rating)">
                    <button type="button" class="btn btn-link btn-sm" @onclick="SortHandler(ReviewSort.Rating)">
                        @Localizer["ReviewRating"]<Icon Name="@SortGlyph(ReviewSort.Rating)" />
                    </button>
                </th>
                <th scope="col">@Localizer["ReviewText"]</th>
                <th scope="col" aria-sort="@SortState(ReviewSort.CreatedAt)">
                    <button type="button" class="btn btn-link btn-sm" @onclick="SortHandler(ReviewSort.CreatedAt)">
                        @Localizer["CreatedAt"]<Icon Name="@SortGlyph(ReviewSort.CreatedAt)" />
                    </button>
                </th>
                @* The actions column keeps its name for a screen reader and drops it on screen, as
                   every other table in the product does: the buttons under it say what they do, and
                   a column named above them competes with them for the same width. *@
                <th scope="col" class="dt-table-actions">
                    <span class="dt-visually-hidden">@Localizer["Actions"]</span>
                </th>
            </HeaderTemplate>
            <RowTemplate Context="review">
                @* The per-cell name, carried by the cell rather than only by the column heading. Not
                   the same thing on both sides of the breakpoint: above it the span is dropped,
                   where the `<th>` already names the column and a second name in every cell would
                   be that name read twice a row; below it the head is gone and the span is painted,
                   as something a reader can see, and that is what replaces it. *@
                <td>
                    <span class="dt-visually-hidden dt-review-label">@Localizer["ReviewDelivery"]</span>
                    @review.DeliveryId
                </td>
                <td>
                    <span class="dt-visually-hidden dt-review-label">@Localizer["ReviewRating"]</span>
                    @* AD-18: the band is Domain's own reading of the number, carried on the row, and
                       the shared display is only what that band looks like. No threshold here, and
                       no colour - the `dt-rating--*` modifier resolves to a --dt-rating-* token in
                       _theme.scss (AD-28, NFR-29). *@
                    <DtRatingDisplay Rating="review.Rating" Band="review.Band" />
                </td>
                <td>
                    <span class="dt-visually-hidden dt-review-label">@Localizer["ReviewText"]</span>
                    @review.Text
                </td>
                <td>
                    <span class="dt-visually-hidden dt-review-label">@Localizer["CreatedAt"]</span>
                    @Moment(review.CreatedAt)
                </td>
                @* `dt-table-actions` rather than a class of this screen's own: the right-aligned,
                   nowrapped actions column is DtDataTable's own rule, and the cell has to wear the
                   class its heading already wears to be reached by it. *@
                <td class="dt-table-actions">
                    <span class="dt-visually-hidden dt-review-label">@Localizer["Actions"]</span>
                    @* The handler is built in C# rather than written as a lambda in the attribute: a
                       `=>` here would close the tag as far as NFR-14's markup scan is concerned, and
                       everything after it would be read as rendered English. *@
                    <button type="button" class="btn btn-secondary btn-sm dt-review-edit" @onclick="EditMine(review)">
                        <Icon Name="IconName.Edit" />@Localizer["Edit"]
                    </button>
                    <button type="button" class="btn btn-danger btn-sm dt-review-delete" @onclick="Remove(review.Id)">
                        <Icon Name="IconName.Delete" />@Localizer["Delete"]
                    </button>
                </td>
            </RowTemplate>
        </DtDataTable>
    }

    @* FR-64: every review, with both parties named. A dispatcher gets the table and no actions,
       which is the same rule the guard states - they read the collection and never write to it -
       rendered rather than restated. *@
    @if (_readsEvery)
    {
        <DtDataTable TItem="ReviewSummary"
                     Page="_allPage"
                     OnPage="GoToPageAsync"
                     OnPageSize="ResizeAsync"
                     CountOne="@Localizer["ReviewsCountOne"]"
                     CountMany="@Localizer["ReviewsCountMany"]"
                     IsLoading="Loading"
                     EmptyText="@Localizer["ReviewsEmpty"]">
            <HeaderTemplate>
                <th scope="col" aria-sort="@SortState(ReviewSort.Delivery)">
                    <button type="button" class="btn btn-link btn-sm" @onclick="SortHandler(ReviewSort.Delivery)">
                        @Localizer["ReviewDelivery"]<Icon Name="@SortGlyph(ReviewSort.Delivery)" />
                    </button>
                </th>
                <th scope="col" aria-sort="@SortState(ReviewSort.Client)">
                    <button type="button" class="btn btn-link btn-sm" @onclick="SortHandler(ReviewSort.Client)">
                        @Localizer["Client"]<Icon Name="@SortGlyph(ReviewSort.Client)" />
                    </button>
                </th>
                <th scope="col" aria-sort="@SortState(ReviewSort.Driver)">
                    <button type="button" class="btn btn-link btn-sm" @onclick="SortHandler(ReviewSort.Driver)">
                        @Localizer["Driver"]<Icon Name="@SortGlyph(ReviewSort.Driver)" />
                    </button>
                </th>
                <th scope="col" aria-sort="@SortState(ReviewSort.Rating)">
                    <button type="button" class="btn btn-link btn-sm" @onclick="SortHandler(ReviewSort.Rating)">
                        @Localizer["ReviewRating"]<Icon Name="@SortGlyph(ReviewSort.Rating)" />
                    </button>
                </th>
                <th scope="col">@Localizer["ReviewText"]</th>
                <th scope="col" aria-sort="@SortState(ReviewSort.CreatedAt)">
                    <button type="button" class="btn btn-link btn-sm" @onclick="SortHandler(ReviewSort.CreatedAt)">
                        @Localizer["CreatedAt"]<Icon Name="@SortGlyph(ReviewSort.CreatedAt)" />
                    </button>
                </th>
                @* Only for the role that has any. A dispatcher reads the collection and writes to
                   none of it, so an unconditional heading here would label a column of permanently
                   empty cells; the cell below carries the same predicate, so header and row stay the
                   same width for whoever is reading. *@
                @if (_moderates)
                {
                    <th scope="col" class="dt-table-actions">
                        <span class="dt-visually-hidden">@Localizer["Actions"]</span>
                    </th>
                }
            </HeaderTemplate>
            <RowTemplate Context="review">
                <td>
                    <span class="dt-visually-hidden dt-review-label">@Localizer["ReviewDelivery"]</span>
                    @review.DeliveryId
                </td>
                <td>
                    <span class="dt-visually-hidden dt-review-label">@Localizer["Client"]</span>
                    @if (review.Client is null)
                    {
                        <span>@Localizer["NoClient"]</span>
                    }
                    @if (review.Client is not null)
                    {
                        <span>@review.Client.Name</span>
                    }
                </td>
                <td>
                    <span class="dt-visually-hidden dt-review-label">@Localizer["Driver"]</span>
                    @if (review.Driver is null)
                    {
                        <span>@Localizer["NoDriver"]</span>
                    }
                    @if (review.Driver is not null)
                    {
                        <span>@review.Driver.Name</span>
                    }
                </td>
                <td>
                    <span class="dt-visually-hidden dt-review-label">@Localizer["ReviewRating"]</span>
                    <DtRatingDisplay Rating="review.Rating" Band="review.Band" />
                </td>
                <td>
                    <span class="dt-visually-hidden dt-review-label">@Localizer["ReviewText"]</span>
                    @review.Text
                </td>
                <td>
                    <span class="dt-visually-hidden dt-review-label">@Localizer["CreatedAt"]</span>
                    @Moment(review.CreatedAt)
                </td>
                @* FR-65 and FR-66 are an administrator's. Offering a dispatcher an action that could
                   only ever fail is worse than not offering it - and FR-12 is unchanged: this hides,
                   the guard decides. The cell goes with the buttons rather than staying behind
                   empty, because the heading above is conditional on the same predicate - and the
                   per-cell name goes with the cell, or a dispatcher's card would carry a line headed
                   "Дії" with nothing at all underneath it. *@
                @if (_moderates)
                {
                    <td class="dt-table-actions">
                        <span class="dt-visually-hidden dt-review-label">@Localizer["Actions"]</span>
                        <button type="button" class="btn btn-secondary btn-sm dt-review-edit" @onclick="EditAny(review)">
                            <Icon Name="IconName.Edit" />@Localizer["Edit"]
                        </button>
                        <button type="button" class="btn btn-danger btn-sm dt-review-delete" @onclick="Remove(review.Id)">
                            <Icon Name="IconName.Delete" />@Localizer["Delete"]
                        </button>
                    </td>
                }
            </RowTemplate>
        </DtDataTable>
    }
</div>

@* A driver. Said plainly rather than shown as an empty table, which would read as "nobody has
   written anything" instead of "this is not yours". Outside the wrapper, because the wrapper is
   an anchor for rules about a table and this is the screen for the one role that gets none. *@
@if (!_authors && !_readsEvery)
{
    <p class="dt-review-refused">@Localizer["ReviewsNotAvailable"]</p>
}

@* FR-85: the one dialog every screen opens, rather than a modal of this screen's own. One heading
   for both writing and editing - a title composed at run time would be a key no test can see, and
   the catalogue is closed against the keys components literally ask for. *@
<DtDialog @ref="_formDialog" Title="@Localizer["ReviewForm"]" OnClosed="CloseForm">
    <ChildContent>
        @* The same list as the one at the top of the page, and deliberately a second copy. A
           DtDialog opens with showModal(), so everything outside it is inert behind the backdrop: a
           refusal rendered only up there is a message nobody can see, and the form would simply
           fail to close.

           It replaces that one rather than joining it: the copy above stands down while this is
           open, so a refused save is one live region announcing once. *@
        @* REVIEW_DELIVERY_NOT_COMPLETED names no field, so it stays here; the scale and the text
           each state their own refusal under themselves. *@
        @if (_formOpen)
        {
            <DtFailureBanner Failures="_failures" InlineFields="@InlineFields" />
        }

        @* Only when writing a new one. A review is keyed one-to-one on its delivery, so moving an
           existing one is deleting it and writing another - there is nothing here to change. *@
        @if (_editingId is null)
        {
            <div class="mb-4">
                <label class="form-label" for="review-delivery">@Localizer["ReviewDelivery"]</label>
                <DtSelect Id="review-delivery" @bind-Value="_form.DeliveryId">
                    @foreach (var delivery in _reviewable)
                    {
                        <option value="@delivery.Id">@Localizer["ReviewDelivery"] @delivery.Id</option>
                    }
                </DtSelect>
                @* FR-62's two halves, said once: only a finished delivery, and only one review
                   each. The list above is already narrowed to both, so this explains why a client
                   who expected to see a parcel here does not. *@
                <div class="form-text">@Localizer["ReviewDeliveryHint"]</div>
            </div>
        }

        <div class="mb-4">
            <label class="form-label" for="review-rating">@Localizer["ReviewRating"]</label>
            @* The options are the scale's own values, so the control cannot offer a number the
               column would refuse (FR-67, AD-18). It opens on RatingScale.Default. *@
            <DtSelect Id="review-rating" @bind-Value="_form.Rating">
                @foreach (var value in Scale)
                {
                    <option value="@value">@value</option>
                }
            </DtSelect>
            <DtFieldError Field="Rating" Failures="_failures" />
        </div>

        <div class="mb-4">
            <label class="form-label" for="review-text">@Localizer["ReviewText"]</label>
            <textarea id="review-text" class="form-control" rows="4"
                      maxlength="@CreateReviewCommandValidator.TextMaximumLength"
                      @bind="_form.Text"></textarea>
            <DtFieldError Field="Text" Failures="_failures" />
        </div>
    </ChildContent>
    <Actions>
        @* Disabled while a save is in flight: the circuit yields at the await, so a second click
           would post the same verdict twice. *@
        <button type="button" class="dt-review-save @SaveVariant" disabled="@_saving"
                @onclick="SaveAsync">
            <Icon Name="@SaveGlyph" />@Localizer["Save"]
        </button>
        <button type="button" class="btn btn-outline-secondary dt-review-cancel" @onclick="CloseFormAsync">
            <Icon Name="IconName.Close" />@Localizer["Cancel"]
        </button>
    </Actions>
</DtDialog>

@* FR-63 and NFR-22: every delete is confirmed, the author's own as much as the moderator's. One
   prompt for both, because it is one destructive action - which is the whole reason the
   confirmation is a shared component rather than a convention each screen remembers. *@
<DtConfirmDialog @ref="_deleteDialog"
                 Title="@Localizer["DeleteReviewTitle"]"
                 Message="@Localizer["DeleteReviewMessage"]"
                 OnConfirmed="DeleteAsync" />

@code {
    /// <summary>
    /// The boxes this screen renders a <c>DtFieldError</c> for. Stated once and bound by every
    /// banner on the page: the list repeated per banner is exactly the drift FR-83 is broken by,
    /// and a screen carries the banner more than once because a dialog opened with showModal()
    /// leaves the page behind it inert.
    /// </summary>
    private const string InlineFields = "Rating,Text";

    /// <summary>
    /// The values the rating control offers: the scale itself, so the form cannot present a number
    /// the check constraint would refuse.
    /// </summary>
    private static readonly int[] Scale =
        [.. Enumerable.Range(RatingScale.Minimum, RatingScale.Maximum - RatingScale.Minimum + 1)];

    private readonly ReviewForm _form = new();

    private DtDialog? _formDialog;
    private DtConfirmDialog? _deleteDialog;

    /// <summary>The author's page, when the caller is a client: the one table they are shown.</summary>
    private Paged<AuthoredReviewSummary>? _minePage;

    /// <summary>The moderation page, when the caller runs dispatch or the system.</summary>
    private Paged<ReviewSummary>? _allPage;
    private IReadOnlyList<AssignedDeliverySummary> _reviewable = [];
    private IReadOnlyList<ScreenFailure> _failures = [];

    /// <summary>
    /// The save action's variant, which follows the mode the dialog is in: creating a record is a
    /// create action and takes the create colour, and changing one that already exists is an edit.
    /// <para>
    /// One dialog serves both modes, so the button is the only thing on screen that can say which
    /// one the reader is in - and AD-28 gives the two actions different colours precisely so that
    /// it does. Resolved here rather than as a ternary in the attribute, for the reason
    /// <c>DtDialog</c> resolves its panel class into a property.
    /// </para>
    /// <para>
    /// Only the variant: the hook stays written out on the button itself, so the source still shows
    /// which control this is - and it is the hook a test names, because asserting the mode by the
    /// variant would be a test that passes the day somebody paints a delete button teal.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    private string SaveVariant => _editingId is null
        ? "btn btn-primary"
        : "btn btn-secondary";

    /// <summary>
    /// The glyph beside it, following the same mode: the create mark when the dialog is making a
    /// record and the confirm mark when it is saving changes to one. The icon and the colour move
    /// together, because colour is never allowed to be the only signal.
    /// </summary>
    private IconName SaveGlyph => _editingId is null ? IconName.Create : IconName.Confirm;

    private int? _editingId;
    private int? _pendingDelete;

    private bool _authors;
    private bool _readsEvery;
    private bool _moderates;

    /// <summary>
    /// True while a save is in flight, which is what disables the save button for as long as it
    /// lasts. The circuit yields at the await, so without it a second click would post the same
    /// verdict twice.
    /// </summary>
    private bool _saving;

    /// <summary>
    /// Whether the write dialog is on screen, which decides where a refusal is rendered.
    /// <para>
    /// Kept here rather than read off the element, because the page needs it while it renders:
    /// exactly one <c>role="alert"</c> region may be in the DOM, or one refusal is announced twice
    /// (NFR-19). Cleared through <c>DtDialog.OnClosed</c>, so Escape and the backdrop count as
    /// closing it just as the cancel action does.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    private bool _formOpen;

    protected override async Task OnInitializedAsync()
    {
        // The circuit outlives the request that opened it, so the cookie can be gone by the time a
        // screen calls a service. Raising the contract's own code here is what puts that case
        // through FR-13's single boundary instead of a per-screen redirect.
        if (!Caller.IsAuthenticated)
        {
            throw new ForbiddenException(
                ErrorCode.AUTH_UNAUTHENTICATED,
                "The circuit has no authenticated caller: the session cookie is gone.");
        }

        // Read once, after the authenticated check: ICurrentUser.Role throws for an anonymous
        // caller, so asking outside that branch would take the screen down instead of refusing it.
        var role = Caller.Role;

        _authors = ReviewViews.Authors(role);
        _readsEvery = ReviewViews.ReadsEveryReview(role);
        _moderates = ReviewViews.Moderates(role);

        await LoadReviewableAsync();

        await base.OnInitializedAsync();
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// A driver reads no review and is shown a sentence instead of a table, so there is nothing to
    /// list; every other role reads exactly one of the two tables.
    /// </summary>
    protected override bool ReadyToList => _authors || _readsEvery;

    /// <summary>Newest first: the review a moderator or an author opens the screen for is the latest.</summary>
    protected override ReviewSort DefaultSort => ReviewSort.CreatedAt;

    /// <inheritdoc />
    protected override bool DefaultDescending => true;

    /// <summary>
    /// The author's table sorts by its three ordered columns; the moderation table adds the two
    /// party names, which the author's list does not carry (AD-17).
    /// </summary>
    protected override IReadOnlyCollection<ReviewSort> Sortable => _readsEvery
        ? [ReviewSort.Delivery, ReviewSort.Client, ReviewSort.Driver, ReviewSort.Rating, ReviewSort.CreatedAt]
        : [ReviewSort.Delivery, ReviewSort.Rating, ReviewSort.CreatedAt];

    /// <inheritdoc />
    protected override ListReviewsQuery Compose(int offset, int limit, ReviewSort sort, bool descending) =>
        new(offset, limit, sort.ToString(), descending);

    /// <inheritdoc />
    protected override (int Offset, int Limit, ReviewSort Sort, bool Descending) Paging(ListReviewsQuery view) =>
        (view.Offset, view.Limit, ListSort.Parse<ReviewSort>(view.Sort) ?? DefaultSort, view.Descending);

    /// <inheritdoc />
    protected override ListReviewsQuery Repaged(
        ListReviewsQuery view,
        int offset,
        int limit,
        ReviewSort sort,
        bool descending) =>
        view with { Offset = offset, Limit = limit, Sort = sort.ToString(), Descending = descending };

    /// <summary>
    /// The role's one table. The two are different row types - the author's carries no party at all
    /// (AD-17) - so the page travels untyped through the shared paging and is split again in
    /// <see cref="OnListed" />.
    /// </summary>
    protected override async Task<Paged<object>?> FetchAsync(ListReviewsQuery view)
    {
        try
        {
            if (_authors)
            {
                return (await Verdicts.ListMineAsync(view, CircuitToken)).Map(review => (object)review);
            }

            return (await Verdicts.ListAsync(view, CircuitToken)).Map(review => (object)review);
        }
        catch (DriveTrackException failure)
        {
            _failures = FailureKeys.For(failure);

            return null;
        }
    }

    /// <inheritdoc />
    protected override void OnListed(Paged<object> page)
    {
        if (_authors)
        {
            _minePage = page.Map(row => (AuthoredReviewSummary)row);
        }

        if (_readsEvery)
        {
            _allPage = page.Map(row => (ReviewSummary)row);
        }
    }

    /// <summary>After a write: the picker's reviewable deliveries and the page on screen, both read again.</summary>
    private async Task ReloadAsync()
    {
        await LoadReviewableAsync();
        await LoadAsync();
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// The deliveries the write form may offer, read unpaged: a delivery already reviewed has to be
    /// left out whichever page of the table its review sits on, and a finished delivery has to be
    /// offered whichever page of the caller's deliveries it would sit on.
    /// </summary>
    private async Task LoadReviewableAsync()
    {
        if (!_authors)
        {
            return;
        }

        try
        {
            _reviewable = await ReviewableAsync(await Verdicts.ListAllMineAsync(CircuitToken));
        }
        catch (DriveTrackException failure)
        {
            _failures = FailureKeys.For(failure);
        }
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// The caller's own deliveries they may still review: finished, and not already reviewed.
    /// <para>
    /// Both halves are the client's convenience rather than the rule. The service refuses an
    /// unfinished delivery with <c>REVIEW_DELIVERY_NOT_COMPLETED</c> and the unique index refuses a
    /// second review with a 409, whatever this list offered — which is what makes it safe to narrow
    /// here without a second authorization decision living on a screen (FR-12).
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    private async Task<IReadOnlyList<AssignedDeliverySummary>> ReviewableAsync(
        IReadOnlyList<AuthoredReviewSummary> written)
    {
        var reviewed = written.Select(review => review.DeliveryId).ToHashSet();

        var own = await Parcels.ListAllMineAsync(CircuitToken);

        return
        [
            .. own.Where(delivery =>
                delivery.Status is DeliveryStatus.Delivered or DeliveryStatus.Failed
                && !reviewed.Contains(delivery.Id)),
        ];
    }

    private Func<Task> EditMine(AuthoredReviewSummary review) =>
        () => OpenEditAsync(review.Id, review.Rating, review.Text);

    private Func<Task> EditAny(ReviewSummary review) =>
        () => OpenEditAsync(review.Id, review.Rating, review.Text);

    private Func<Task> Remove(int id) => () => OpenDeleteAsync(id);

    private async Task OpenCreateAsync()
    {
        _editingId = null;
        _failures = [];

        // Preselected rather than left blank: an unset picker submits no delivery, and "no
        // delivery" is a 404 from the scoped read - a true answer to a question the client did not
        // mean to ask.
        _form.Reset(_reviewable.Count > 0 ? _reviewable[0].Id : null);

        _formOpen = true;

        if (_formDialog is not null)
        {
            await _formDialog.ShowAsync();
        }
    }

    private async Task OpenEditAsync(int id, int rating, string text)
    {
        _editingId = id;
        _failures = [];

        _form.Fill(rating, text);

        _formOpen = true;

        if (_formDialog is not null)
        {
            await _formDialog.ShowAsync();
        }
    }

    private async Task OpenDeleteAsync(int id)
    {
        _pendingDelete = id;
        _failures = [];

        if (_deleteDialog is not null)
        {
            await _deleteDialog.ShowAsync();
        }
    }

    private async Task SaveAsync()
    {
        if (_saving)
        {
            return;
        }

        // Set before the await and cleared in the finally: the button is disabled from the moment
        // the first click is handled until the server has answered, so a second click has nothing
        // to act on.
        _saving = true;

        try
        {
            if (_editingId is { } id)
            {
                // AD-23: both fields are sent because both are on the form. Absent would mean
                // "leave it alone", which is not what a filled-in form says.
                await Verdicts.UpdateAsync(
                    id,
                    new UpdateReviewCommand(
                        Optional<int>.Present(_form.Rating),
                        Optional<string>.Present(_form.Text)),
                    CircuitToken);
            }

            if (_editingId is null)
            {
                await Verdicts.CreateAsync(
                    new CreateReviewCommand(_form.DeliveryId ?? 0, _form.Rating, _form.Text),
                    CircuitToken);
            }
        }
        catch (DriveTrackException failure)
        {
            _failures = FailureKeys.For(failure);

            return;
        }
        finally
        {
            _saving = false;
        }

        // Cleared on the way out, not only when a dialog opens: the banner is rendered at page
        // level as well as inside the dialog, so a refused attempt that is then corrected and
        // accepted would otherwise leave the failure standing on the page behind the closed
        // dialog, contradicting the row the user can see was written.
        _failures = [];

        // Outside the try, because the write has already committed by now: a refetch that failed
        // inside it would be caught and rendered as though the write had been refused, which is the
        // one thing the user must not be told.
        await CloseFormAsync();
        await ReloadAsync();
    }

    private async Task DeleteAsync()
    {
        if (_pendingDelete is not { } id)
        {
            return;
        }

        try
        {
            await Verdicts.DeleteAsync(id, CircuitToken);
        }
        catch (DriveTrackException failure)
        {
            _failures = FailureKeys.For(failure);

            return;
        }

        // Cleared here too: a refused delete followed by a confirmed one would otherwise leave the
        // refusal standing above a table the row is already gone from.
        _failures = [];
        _pendingDelete = null;

        await ReloadAsync();
    }

    private async Task CloseFormAsync()
    {
        if (_formDialog is not null)
        {
            await _formDialog.CloseAsync();
        }
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Raised once for every dismissal — the cancel action, Escape or the backdrop — which is why
    /// the flag is cleared here and not in <see cref="CloseFormAsync"/>: a dialog dismissed by the
    /// platform would otherwise leave the page with no live region at all.
    /// </summary>
    /// <summary>
    /// Stands the dialog's banner down, and drops the refusals with it (FR-83).
    /// <para>
    /// The list has to go too, not just the flag. A refusal claimed by the scale or the text is
    /// subtracted from the page-level banner this restores, so leaving it populated would leave
    /// that sentence with nowhere on the page to render.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    private void CloseForm()
    {
        _formOpen = false;
        _failures = [];
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// An instant as this screen shows it. Local time against the current culture (NFR-15); the
    /// circuit runs interactively on the server, so <c>ToLocalTime</c> resolves against the host -
    /// the same reading every other screen in the product gives a stored instant.
    /// </summary>
    private static string Moment(DateTimeOffset value) =>
        value.ToLocalTime().ToString("g", System.Globalization.CultureInfo.CurrentCulture);

    /// <summary>
    /// What the dialog is bound to. A form object rather than the command itself: a command is what
    /// is sent once the boxes are filled, and a half-filled box is not a value the contract has to
    /// be able to carry.
    /// </summary>
    private sealed class ReviewForm
    {
        /// <summary>The delivery being reviewed. Null only before the first delivery is offered.</summary>
        public int? DeliveryId { get; set; }

        /// <summary>The score. Opens on the scale's default rather than on zero, which is off it.</summary>
        public int Rating { get; set; } = RatingScale.Default;

        /// <summary>The comment.</summary>
        public string? Text { get; set; }

        /// <summary>Clears the form for a new review, aimed at the delivery given.</summary>
        public void Reset(int? deliveryId)
        {
            DeliveryId = deliveryId;
            Rating = RatingScale.Default;
            Text = null;
        }

        /// <summary>Fills the form from a stored review.</summary>
        public void Fill(int rating, string text)
        {
            DeliveryId = null;
            Rating = rating;
            Text = text;
        }
    }
}
